WEBVTT

00:03.200 --> 00:06.272
Small teams post the same job on four job sites,

00:06.292 --> 00:08.760
and get a pile of applications back.

00:09.000 --> 00:12.261
But they never find out which job post brought the person they hired.

00:13.500 --> 00:17.888
recruited.tools is an open-source applicant tracker that answers that.

00:17.908 --> 00:20.973
The demo runs on sample data from a music agency.

00:20.993 --> 00:23.880
Three jobs, seven job posts, four job sites.

00:23.900 --> 00:26.327
The home screen doesn't lead with applications.

00:26.347 --> 00:28.787
It leads with what an A-tier candidate cost.

00:30.600 --> 00:34.578
Same job site, same spend. Variant A got twice the applications.

00:34.598 --> 00:37.952
Variant B paid more, and named the tools in the title.

00:37.972 --> 00:41.245
It brought 3.7× more A-tier candidates per dollar.

00:43.600 --> 00:45.767
Every variant gets its own apply page,

00:45.787 --> 00:49.044
so each application knows which job post it came from.

00:49.900 --> 00:53.001
Every applicant, from every job site, lands on one pipeline,

00:53.021 --> 00:55.356
tagged with the variant that brought them.

00:57.300 --> 01:00.322
Interviewers score on their own, and the summary is drawn only

01:00.342 --> 01:03.092
from what the candidate sent and what the team wrote.

01:04.300 --> 01:07.861
Posting a job means writing two variants and picking the job sites.

01:07.881 --> 01:09.849
The test starts when it goes live.

01:11.800 --> 01:14.502
It's open source, and it deploys in five minutes.

01:14.522 --> 01:17.616
Hire from the job post that brings A-tier candidates.
